 confronted by this situation and as he  put it in his letter to Truman an iron  curtain is drawn down on the front the  phrase he was later to use two years  later at Fulton Missouri or a year later  at Fulton Missouri so that was really  the as he saw his Churchill sword in  retrospect the not the failure of the  war because he was a great believer that  you had to defeat Germany you couldn't  start playing games while Germany was  still undefeated with regard to the  post-war world because there would be no  post-war world unless Germany was  defeated and the Soviet Union as he and  Roosevelt both appreciated and as  Churchill certainly referred to a great  deal and also in their correspondence  was putting by far the largest number of  men in the field at any given time and  for long periods of the war were the  only soldiers effectively fighting on  any substantial war front so that was a  failure but I think you just have to say  that the defeat of Hitler was was the  success that mattered at that time
 

